---
source: crates/pse-host/src/cli.rs
expression: "format!(\"-h\\n{short}\\n--help\\n{long}\")"
---
-h
Usage: pse run [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>...

Arguments:
  <COMMAND>...

Options:
  -C <checkout>

      --detach
          Keep the owned invocation alive after this launching process returns
      --format <FORMAT>
          Human output on terminals, JSON on pipes, or compact NDJSON observations [env: PSE_FORMAT] [default: auto] [possible values: auto, human, json, ndjson]
      --session <SESSION>
          Explicit session association; never used as a filesystem path
      --fields <FIELDS>
          Select comma-separated payload fields while retaining identity, state and coverage
      --resource-class <RESOURCE_CLASS>
          Explicit finite workload class; ordinary work keeps the configured host policy
      --budget <BUDGET>
          Limit payload array records; retain complete evidence as an artifact
  -h, --help
          Print help (see more with '--help')
--help
Usage: pse run [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>...

Arguments:
  <COMMAND>...


Options:
  -C <checkout>


      --detach
          Keep the owned invocation alive after this launching process returns

      --format <FORMAT>
          Human output on terminals, JSON on pipes, or compact NDJSON observations

          Possible values:
          - auto:   Human for a terminal, JSON for pipes
          - human:  Concise summary and pretty structured detail
          - json:   One JSON envelope
          - ndjson: Compact normalized records followed by an observation summary

          [env: PSE_FORMAT]
          [default: auto]

      --session <SESSION>
          Explicit session association; never used as a filesystem path

      --fields <FIELDS>
          Select comma-separated payload fields while retaining identity, state and coverage

      --resource-class <RESOURCE_CLASS>
          Explicit finite workload class; ordinary work keeps the configured host policy

      --budget <BUDGET>
          Limit payload array records; retain complete evidence as an artifact

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
